I noticed some people pulling this old code.

ISSUE: It uses <time.h> to seed rand()

From --- char **split_args(int argc, char **args) -- and -- ./a.out (-)<num_of_digits> <size of array>  <sets of arrays> <name of file> we get

To shell call:

EXE (-)<num_of_digits>*<size of array>*<sets of arrays>*<name of file>
(blank space required at *)

* (-) add it to include negative numbers
* <num_of_digits> in each value
* <size of array> values on a line
* <sets of arrays> divided by a new line, dont fill if un-needed
* <name of file> optional, MUST be aplpha chars, uses open to drop a text file/overwrite.

output string: (-)<digits>*<digits>*.......*<newline>

<<<<<<<<THE ISSUE>>>>>>>>

The issue: To avoid the same output being produced ie too many recalls per second, creating sets of tests the same,
there is rs = 7, as an off set, and 7877 hard coded. 

static int rs = 7;
rs = rs + 7877;

<<<<<<[ However! ]>>>>>>

if you shell call this EXE 7 seconds later, or 7870 secs(2 Hours : 11 Minutes : 10 Seconds) later, 
it will produce the same output for 1 set/test of numbers!
